WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.998 --> 00:00:04.491
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.730
If we were to
imagine what it would

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:26.020
look like to build
a whole self at MIT,

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:27.960
I think it starts with music.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:33.240
Music is where you find
your personal expression.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.820
Music is what
opens new horizons.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.520
I was very, very
excited when Fred Harris

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.910
contacted me to be a virtual
visiting artist at MIT.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:48.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:52.160
Anat Cohen is a
treasure of a musician.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.790
She crosses many
boundaries, plays

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:59.080
jazz clarinet, also saxophone,
got interested in choro.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.720
Choro is a type
of Brazilian music

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.970
that grew up around
Rio de Janeiro

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.269
in the early 20th century.

00:01:06.269 --> 00:01:09.263
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.110
I got to write an arrangement
of a Brazilian choro called

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:15.980
"Cheguei."

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.200
And they recorded
it and performed it

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.250
in a virtual concert.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:21.710
And I got to attend a
couple of rehearsals

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:22.970
and work with the students.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:24.930
And it was a great
experience for me.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.590
And I hope-- by
some of the smiles

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:32.252
I could see that those aha
moments were really nice.

00:01:32.252 --> 00:01:36.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.810
I could see how a
student went from point A

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.150
to point B. How the
progress was made.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.980
How more relaxed they were.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:44.570
How they may have
taken my advice,

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:46.310
but also how to just
work on their craft

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.320
and achieve the next level.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.590
It felt like a very
inclusive process.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:55.530
What's most impactful is
like the direct feedback.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.530
We'd play something and there
would be those tiny things

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.360
that you might not
notice if you were just

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.733
listening to a recording
and try to emulate it.

00:02:03.733 --> 00:02:06.150
She'd be, like, oh, you know,
you're rushing a little bit,

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.520
or she'd be, like, oh,
you have to introduce

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.680
this slightly different
swing that you're maybe not

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.352
used to playing.

00:02:12.352 --> 00:02:15.789
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.630
I met all these incredible
students of MIT and realizing

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:30.400
how important music is for them.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.890
How serious they are about it.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:34.210
And how dedicated they are.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.520
And playing and keep
being involved and playing

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:41.020
with other musicians is
important for their existence.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:44.370
[MUSIC PLAYING]

